<!-- metadoc:file version="0" source="token_bucket.py" -->
# token_bucket.py

A **token-bucket rate limiter**: events may arrive in short bursts, but over
any longer interval their average rate is capped. The clock is injected, so
the limiter can be driven by real time in production and by a hand-advanced
fake clock in tests and in the demo at the bottom of the file.

<!-- metadoc:section symbol="TokenBucket" lines="10-44" hash="2633f4859988" -->
## `TokenBucket`

The bucket holds up to `capacity` tokens and refills continuously at `rate`
tokens per second. Each event spends tokens; when the bucket is empty, events
are refused until enough time has passed. Two parameters therefore shape the
traffic independently: `capacity` is the largest burst, `rate` the long-run
average.

`tokens` and `stamp` (the time of the last refill) are internal state and are
excluded from the constructor.

<!-- metadoc:section symbol="TokenBucket.__post_init__" lines="20-24" hash="41934af911f5" -->
### Construction

Rejects non-positive `rate` or `capacity`, which would make the bucket either
never refill or never hold a token. A new bucket starts **full**, so the first
burst is granted immediately.

<!-- metadoc:section symbol="TokenBucket._refill" lines="27-30" hash="4afc378e8ed3" -->
### `_refill` — lazy top-up

Instead of a timer, the bucket credits `elapsed · rate` tokens whenever it is
queried, capped at `capacity`. The cap is what limits bursts: an idle period
cannot bank more than one full bucket.

<!-- metadoc:section symbol="TokenBucket.try_acquire" lines="32-39" hash="238c663b3b0e" -->
### `try_acquire` — non-blocking take

Refills, then spends `cost` tokens if they are available and reports whether
the event may proceed. It never waits. A `cost` above `capacity` raises
instead of returning `False` forever, because the bucket can never hold that
many tokens.

<!-- metadoc:section symbol="TokenBucket.wait_time" lines="41-44" hash="30e259b2531d" -->
### `wait_time` — when to retry

Seconds until `cost` tokens will be available, assuming nobody else takes
tokens in the meantime; `0.0` if they are available now. Callers that want to
block can sleep for this long and then call `try_acquire`.

<!-- metadoc:section symbol="FakeClock" lines="47-54" hash="5c0c11f6dbae" -->
## `FakeClock`

A callable that returns a time the caller sets by hand (`clock.now += 0.25`).
Passing it as `clock` makes the bucket's behaviour fully deterministic.

<!-- metadoc:section lines="57-62" hash="4b9bcd35902f" -->
## Demo

Attempts one event every 0.25 s against a bucket with `capacity=3` and
`rate=2/s`, i.e. twice as fast as the bucket allows. The first events drain
the initial burst, after which roughly every other attempt is refused.
